  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe aim to improve the current C\u2085 bound by employing a structured initial guess and refined optimization strategy. The core idea is to use a symmetric, periodic initial guess inspired by signal processing principles to balance overlaps across lags. This reduces the risk of local minima and enables more effective optimization. The primary bottleneck is the previous approach's reliance on random initial guesses, which may not exploit geometric properties of the problem space. By using a well-defined, low-overlap pattern as a starting point, we increase the likelihood of finding globally better solutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx\nsubject to:\n- 0 \u2264 h[x] \u2264 1 for all x\n- \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nThis is equivalent to ensuring the autocorrelation of h(x) is minimized for all lags. We represent the solution as a piecewise-constant function with n_points samples over [0, 2], where dx = 2/n_points. The integral constraint is enforced through a scaling factor: sum(h) = n_points/2 exactly. We use the convolution-based objective: max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx).\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation\n- If initial_h_values is provided, convert to NumPy array. Otherwise, generate a structured initial guess.\n- Compute dx = 2 / n_points, target_sum = n_points / 2.\n\nStage 2: Initial guess construction\n- Generate a symmetric, periodic initial guess using a sawtooth wave pattern: h_initial[i] = 1 - |(i - n_points/2)/n_points| * 2, then clip between 0 and 1.\n- This creates a V-shaped function with minimum overlap for nearby shifts.\n\nStage 3: Optimization loop\n- Use SLSQP with bounds [0,1] and constraint sum(h) = target_sum.\n- For each iteration:\n   - Compute objective function as max correlation.\n   - Update h via gradient descent with momentum, adding noise to escape local minima.\n   - Re-scale h to maintain integral constraint.\n- Track best solution found with minimal C\u2085.\n\nStage 4: Post-processing\n- Perform a final refinement using L-BFGS-B for precision.\n- Return best h, C\u2085, and n_points.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 400 (tested for accuracy vs speed)\n- Initial guess: symmetric sawtooth pattern\n- Optimization: SLSQP with L-BFGS-B refinement\n- Noise injection: 5% random perturbations every 20 iterations\n- Maximum iterations: 500 (with early termination if budget is exceeded)\n\nVariations to consider:\n- Reduce n_points to 200 for faster execution while preserving symmetry\n- Switch to a sine-based initial guess for different overlap patterns\n- Use differential evolution for global exploration when stuck\n- Adjust perturbation magnitude based on objective improvement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime estimate:\n- Initial guess: O(1)\n- Optimization loop: 500 iterations * O(n_points) = 500*400 = 200,000 operations\n- Refinement: O(1) \n\nMemory: O(n_points) for h, small due to sparse updates.\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Check for NaNs in objective function\n- Clip h to [0,1] after each update\n- Reserve fallback to initial guess if optimization fails\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Validate 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Ensure sum(h) == n_points/2 exactly\n- Compute objective using original correlate function\n\nSanity checks:\n- Ensure C\u2085 is scalar and finite\n- Verify that all h values are within [0,1]\n- Re-evaluate final solution using the exact task metric",
